# Source footage and stills live on disk only — ~1 GB of camera material that
# has no business in git. The animation reads them by path; it never needs them
# committed. Keep them backed up separately.
#
# These rules only bind UNTRACKED files: the media was committed anyway and had
# to be removed from the index with `git rm --cached` before they took effect.
# It is still in history — see the repo's open item on rewriting that.
/*.mp4
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.png
/*.JPG

# Video editor scratch
widevideo/cache/

# Render intermediates: hundreds of PNGs per pass, regenerated by
# `npm run render:intro` / `npm run render:outro`.
animation/*/frames/

# Footage plates: the live-action frames each piece composites over, pulled out
# of the source clips by `npm run plates:intro` / `npm run plates:outro`.
# Hundreds of MB and fully regenerable, but only on a machine that has the
# source footage.
animation/*/plates/

# The rendered pieces. The intro used to be tracked, back when it was 3 MB of
# pure graphics and worth having in a fresh clone. Once real footage went into
# the render it became ~90 MB of churn — a fresh blob in history on every pass
# — which is the exact habit that put 1.5 GB into this repo's history once
# already. Both are fully regenerable:
#   npm run plates:intro && npm run render:intro
animation/*/*.mp4

node_modules/
npm-debug.log*

# Local Claude Code permission allowlist — machine-specific, not shared config.
.claude/settings.local.json
